---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const ASK = 'https://github.com/cookc/steadyship/issues/new?title=I%20want%3A%20&body=What%20would%20you%20want%20Steadyship%20to%20do%20first%2C%20and%20for%20what%20kind%20of%20business%3F';

const groups = [
  {
    heading: 'Before anything is built',
    intro: 'The questions an engineer would ask. Never a questionnaire, never more than two.',
    items: [
      ['Asks what you don\'t know to ask', 'You say "Make the job board track hours." It has read your build guide, your other apps and your spreadsheets, so it asks: against the whole job, or each step? Where should this live so the team can see it? Isn\'t this already in the price list? It recommends an answer, you decide, it builds.'],
      ['Checks the ground first', 'Most of what goes wrong with home-made apps is the setup around them. Say "Check this computer" and in one minute it tells you whether it can reach GitHub, read and write the shared drive your apps depend on, and open one of your apps. PASS or FAIL, written down, so nobody has to wonder again.'],
      ['Explains any complexity it adds, in one line', 'A library, a test, a shared file: each comes with one plain sentence on why it is worth it, and more if you ask. You should never feel it got slower for no reason.'],
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'After it is built',
    intro: 'The opinion nobody was there to give.',
    items: [
      ['A straight verdict', 'Ask "Review the quote builder" and you get Yes, No or Not yet, in the first line. Then the stage it is at, the five places it disagrees with your master spreadsheet, the three biggest risks, and the one next step. Every claim names its file and its row.'],
      ['Security in plain words', 'Who can see this app? Where does what people type end up? Is a password in a file? Is the shared drive open to anyone with the link? It names each exposure and what closing it would take. It never calls an app secure; it says what it checked and labels what it could not.'],
      ['A second reviewer, and one from another vendor', 'A separate, independent reviewer checks every verdict. For a business that wants the strongest check, the brief and the change can go to a reviewer from a different AI vendor, so it does not share the builder\'s blind spots. No terminal, no copy-pasting.'],
      ['Honest about its limits', 'When something is beyond its evidence, a platform problem or a security question it cannot verify, it says so and names what a human engineer should be asked.'],
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'The path',
    intro: 'From a prototype to something the business runs on, one step at a time. Never a rebuild.',
    items: [
      ['The whole picture on one page', 'What apps you have, what each is for, who relies on it, where its data is, what it duplicates, how exposed it is. Written so the owner can read it in five minutes.'],
      ['Four stages, and it says which', 'Prototype: one file, one person\'s tool. Owned tool: data in one shared, backed-up place with one writer, a test, a named owner. Proper app: a real framework and shared parts. Platform: several apps on one database, kept in step. An app\'s label changes only when the evidence does.'],
      ['It makes the call, you can defer', 'You never choose a framework, a database or a host. When an app has reached the point where it should become a proper app, it says so, gives the reason, and offers to do it now or later. The decision is recorded either way.'],
      ['Rigour when it\'s needed, not before', 'A shared module, a package or a database is introduced only when duplication or data divergence is shown. Before any restructuring it keeps a working baseline and writes a behavioural test. Every step is small and reversible.'],
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'Apps that stay in step',
    intro: 'The fix for the pile getting bigger.',
    items: [
      ['One master for everything', 'It finds where the same price, job or customer is typed into two apps and a spreadsheet, shows where they already disagree, and points every app at the one file that is the real master.'],
      ['A shared glossary with a human owner', 'What a "job" is, how its number is written, what the stages are called. Every new piece of work is checked against it.'],
      ['A template for new apps', 'So coherence is the starting point: the glossary, the data contracts and a test are there before the first line is written.'],
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'Everything stays yours',
    intro: 'You are never worse off for having used it.',
    items: [
      ['Ordinary files in a folder the business owns', 'Markdown, your apps, scripts and tests. No proprietary format, no hosted dependency, nothing stored anywhere else.'],
      ['Private by construction', 'Repositories it creates are private, and it refuses to push to one that is not. Passwords, secrets and large files never enter the folder.'],
      ['Your data, your call', 'Whether business data may go to a cloud session is the business\'s decision. It asks once, records the answer, and adapts.'],
      ['Remove it tomorrow', 'Everything it wrote is still there and still useful. Its honest answer is sometimes "use the tool you already pay for", and that is it working.'],
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base title="Features" description="Everything Steadyship does: asks before building, gives a straight verdict after, names security exposures in plain words, moves apps from prototype to something the business runs on, and keeps every app reading the same master data.">
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
    <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sea">Features</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-extrabold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">An engineer working with you, always.</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
      Every app your business builds with Claude should have an engineer beside it: asking before, judging after,
      watching for the moment it starts to matter, and then bringing in exactly as much engineering as that moment needs.
      Here is what that looks like.
    </p>

    {groups.map((g) => (
      <section class="mt-16">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">{g.heading}</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--muted)]">{g.intro}</p>
        <dl class="mt-6 divide-y divide-[var(--line)] rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)]">
          {g.items.map(([k, v]) => (
            <div class="p-5">
              <dt class="font-display text-lg font-bold">{k}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">{v}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </section>
    ))}

    <div class="mt-16 rounded-3xl bg-navy px-6 py-10 text-center text-white dark:border dark:border-white/10 sm:px-12">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Which of these would you want first?</h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-xl text-[#C6D3DE]">Steadyship is being built with real businesses, feature by feature. Tell us what yours needs and what kind of business it is.</p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href={ASK} class="inline-block rounded-full bg-sun px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-navy hover:bg-[#F7C95F]">Tell us what you'd want</a>
        <a href="/get-started" class="inline-block rounded-full border border-white/30 px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-white hover:border-white">Get started</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
